##
    Where a throwaway is refused.

    This program does not compile. A throwaway receives a value nobody wants to keep, and every
    place it is allowed is invisible from outside the body it sits in — a local, a loop's
    element or counter, a caught exception. samples/throwaway.pc holds that side.

    Everywhere else it is refused, and for two different reasons. Reading one asks for a value
    that was thrown away. Naming something with one leaves that thing unreachable, since the
    only way back to a field, a function, a type or an enumeration member is to write its name.

    A parameter is refused for a third reason: it is part of a signature somebody else reads to
    work out what to pass, and it is shown to them at every call. A function that has no use for
    an argument should not ask for one.

    The last mistake here is not a throwaway at all. A local nothing ever reads is worth saying
    out loud, because a result worked out and then forgotten looks exactly like one meant to be
    dropped — and writing '_' is how a program tells the two apart.
##

shared model Program

    # A field is reached by writing its name, so one called '_' could never be read.
    shared integer _ = 3;

    # The same for a function, and for each of its parameters. A caller reading this signature
    # is told the first argument matters enough to pass and not enough to name.
    shared function Draw(integer _, integer height)
        Console.WriteLine(height);
    end function

    shared function Main()
        let _ = 41;

        # Nothing was kept, so there is nothing here to read or to hand on.
        Console.WriteLine(_);
        Program.Draw(_, 2);

        # A throwaway is the value it drops. With no value it drops nothing.
        integer _;

        # A lambda's parameter is a parameter. Where its argument is of no interest, the lambda
        # is usually not where the work belongs.
        integer delegate(integer) one = function(_)
            yield 1;
        end function;

        Console.WriteLine(one(5));

        # Not a throwaway, and not a refusal: a warning. Nothing reads this, so either it was
        # meant to be used or it was meant to be dropped, and only one of those is written down.
        integer forgotten = 9;
    end function
end model

model Shelf
    # A function called '_' is unreachable for the same reason a field is.
    function _()
        Console.WriteLine("nothing can call this");
    end function

    # The same warning, one level out: a private member nothing reaches. Private is the only
    # visibility it can be said of, since a wider one may be reached from somewhere else.
    integer capacity = 12;

    public function Describe()
        Console.WriteLine("a shelf");
    end function
end model

# A type and its members are reached by name, and so is a namespace.
namespace _
    model _
    end model

    enumeration Weekday
        _
    end enumeration
end namespace
